{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChDFGBwV3LcmNCFZMzCYE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I marked `samples/s195.c` as **not vulnerable** and wrote the answer to `results/s195.json`.\n\nThe function is a user-space kernel self-test (`close_range_bitmap_corruption`), not kernel code. Here is what it does:\n1. Opens file descriptors 2 to 127.\n2. Creates a child process that shares the parent's descriptor table.\n3. In the child, calls `close_range(64, ~0U, CLOSE_RANGE_UNSHARE)` so the child gets its own copy of the table with descriptors 64 and up closed.\n4. Checks that `dup(0)` then returns 64. If it doesn't, the kernel's internal record of which descriptors are in use is wrong.\n\nThe test only makes system calls and checks their results, so it contains no memory-safety or logic flaw of its own. It exists to catch a kernel bug of that kind.\n\nOne line looks wrong but isn't: `EXPECT_EQ(dup(0), 64)` has no semicolon before `exit(EXIT_FAILURE);`. The kselftest check macros let you put a statement right after them that runs only when the check fails, so this is valid code."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":694,"cache_read_input_tokens":22396,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":694},"output_tokens":380,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"24c1db01-f12d-42db-b352-b5f2593828ac","uuid":"283563a6-c368-4c0a-b614-ae15d2ec5b28","timestamp":"2026-11-20T00:38:34.149Z","request_id":"req_011ChDFGBo3jfxJZSnBQo5FN"}
